### What is fault isolation and why does it matter?

Fault isolation is the practice of containing a failure to the smallest possible boundary so it cannot cascade into a system-wide outage. In microservices, failures are inevitable: services crash, networks partition, dependencies slow down. The question is not whether failures happen but whether one failure takes down everything.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class CascadeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Without fault isolation:");
        System.out.println("  Statement service slows -> shared thread pool exhausted");
        System.out.println("  Transfer service has no threads -> transfers fail");
        System.out.println("  Balance lookups time out -> entire bank is down");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("With fault isolation:");
        System.out.println("  Statement service slows -> only statement bulkhead exhausted");
        System.out.println("  Transfer service has its own pool -> transfers work fine");
        System.out.println("  Balance lookups have their own pool -> balances work fine");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Without fault isolation:
  Statement service slows -> shared thread pool exhausted
  Transfer service has no threads -> transfers fail
  Balance lookups time out -> entire bank is down

With fault isolation:
  Statement service slows -> only statement bulkhead exhausted
  Transfer service has its own pool -> transfers work fine
  Balance lookups have their own pool -> balances work fine
```

### How does exception handling provide isolation?

The goal is to catch failures at the right place, translate low-level errors into meaningful domain errors, and never let an exception propagate uncontrolled across a boundary.

```java
package com.bank.exception;

import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ExceptionHandler;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestControllerAdvice;

@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(InsufficientBalanceException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleInsufficientBalance(InsufficientBalanceException e) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY)
                .body(new ErrorResponse("INSUFFICIENT_BALANCE", e.getMessage()));
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleGeneral(Exception e) {
        // Log the full stack trace internally but return a clean error
        System.err.println("Internal error: " + e.getMessage());
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR)
                .body(new ErrorResponse("INTERNAL_ERROR", "An unexpected error occurred"));
    }

    record ErrorResponse(String code, String message) {}
}
```

```text
Expected output (insufficient balance):
{"code":"INSUFFICIENT_BALANCE","message":"Account SAV2001 has insufficient funds"}

Expected output (database failure):
{"code":"INTERNAL_ERROR","message":"An unexpected error occurred"}
```

### How do microservice boundaries isolate faults?

By splitting the system into services that each own their data and communicate through interfaces, a failure in one cannot directly corrupt another's state. If the statement service crashes, the transfer service keeps working.

```java
package com.bank.boundary;

public class ServiceBoundaryDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Service boundaries as firewalls:");
        System.out.println("  transfer-service: owns transfers table");
        System.out.println("  statement-service: owns statements table");
        System.out.println("  No shared database tables, no shared in-process memory");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("When statement-service crashes:");
        System.out.println("  transfer-service: UNAFFECTED (different process, different DB)");
        System.out.println("  Rajesh can still transfer money");
        System.out.println("  Rajesh cannot view statements (graceful degradation)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Service boundaries as firewalls:
  transfer-service: owns transfers table
  statement-service: owns statements table
  No shared database tables, no shared in-process memory

When statement-service crashes:
  transfer-service: UNAFFECTED (different process, different DB)
  Rajesh can still transfer money
  Rajesh cannot view statements (graceful degradation)
```

### How do bulkheads partition resources?

Bulkheads give each dependency or workload its own bounded thread pool or connection pool. If calls to a slow fraud service pile up, they exhaust only the fraud bulkhead's threads and the threads serving transfers stay free.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import io.github.resilience4j.bulkhead.annotation.Bulkhead;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class FraudCheckClient {

    @Bulkhead(name = "fraudCheck", fallbackMethod = "fallback")
    public String checkFraud(String accountId) {
        // If this call is slow, only the fraudCheck bulkhead's
        // 10 concurrent calls are consumed, not the global pool
        return externalFraudService.check(accountId);
    }

    public String fallback(String accountId, Throwable t) {
        System.out.println("Fraud bulkhead full for " + accountId + ", using fallback");
        return "MANUAL_REVIEW";
    }
}
```

```yaml
# Resilience4j bulkhead configuration
resilience4j:
  bulkhead:
    instances:
      fraudCheck:
        maxConcurrentCalls: 10
        maxWaitDuration: 500ms
      statementService:
        maxConcurrentCalls: 20
        maxWaitDuration: 1s
```

```text
Expected output (when fraud service is slow):
Fraud bulkhead full for SAV2001, using fallback
// Transfer threads are UNAFFECTED because they use a different bulkhead
```

### How do circuit breakers isolate in time?

When a downstream service is clearly down, the breaker opens so further calls fail fast instead of piling up, protecting both the caller's threads and the struggling callee.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.annotation.CircuitBreaker;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class CreditScoreClient {

    @CircuitBreaker(name = "creditScore", fallbackMethod = "fallbackScore")
    public int getCreditScore(String accountId) {
        // When this fails repeatedly, the circuit opens
        // and calls fail fast without touching the downstream
        return externalCreditService.getScore(accountId);
    }

    public int fallbackScore(String accountId, Throwable t) {
        System.out.println("Circuit open for credit score, returning default for " + accountId);
        return 650; // Safe default score
    }
}
```

```yaml
resilience4j:
  circuitbreaker:
    instances:
      creditScore:
        slidingWindowSize: 10
        failureRateThreshold: 50
        waitDurationInOpenState: 30s
        permittedNumberOfCallsInHalfOpenState: 3
```

```text
Expected output (circuit open):
Circuit open for credit score, returning default for SAV2001
```

### How do Kubernetes resource limits enforce isolation?

CPU and memory limits per container ensure a memory leak in one pod triggers only that pod's termination, not starvation of its neighbors on the same node.

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: payment-service
spec:
  containers:
    - name: payment
      image: nbi/payment-service:1.0
      resources:
        requests:
          memory: "512Mi"
          cpu: "250m"
        limits:
          memory: "1Gi"
          cpu: "500m"
      # If this container exceeds 1Gi memory -> OOMKilled
      # If this container tries to use > 500m CPU -> throttled
      # Neighboring containers on the same node are UNAFFECTED
```

```text
Memory leak scenario:
  payment-service memory grows to 1.1Gi -> OOMKilled by kubelet
  account-service on same node -> UNAFFECTED (has its own limits)
  Kubernetes restarts payment-service automatically
```

### How do redundancy and failover isolate location failures?

Running multiple instances across nodes and zones, with automatic failover to survivors, means the loss of one does not remove the function. For stateful components like databases, failover to a replica preserves the data path.

```java
package com.bank.failover;

public class FailoverDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Location fault isolation:");
        System.out.println("  3 pods across 3 zones");
        System.out.println("  Zone ap-south-1a goes down -> 1 pod lost");
        System.out.println("  2 pods in other zones continue serving");
        System.out.println("  Kubernetes recreates the lost pod in a healthy zone");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Database primary in zone-a fails");
        System.out.println("  Synchronous replica in zone-b promoted to primary");
        System.out.println("  Application reconnects automatically");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Location fault isolation:
  3 pods across 3 zones
  Zone ap-south-1a goes down -> 1 pod lost
  2 pods in other zones continue serving
  Kubernetes recreates the lost pod in a healthy zone

  Database primary in zone-a fails
  Synchronous replica in zone-b promoted to primary
  Application reconnects automatically
```

### What should a senior engineer know about defence in depth?

Fault isolation is defence in depth across three dimensions: space (bulkheads, boundaries, resource limits), time (circuit breakers, timeouts), and location (redundancy, failover). No single dimension is sufficient alone.

| Dimension | Technique | What it isolates |
|---|---|---|
| Space | Bulkhead, microservice boundary, resource limits | One workload from consuming all shared resources |
| Time | Circuit breaker, timeout | A slow or dead dependency from piling up callers |
| Location | Multi-zone, failover, redundancy | A node or zone failure from removing a function |

```text
Defence in depth:
  Space:    Bulkheads + boundaries + resource limits
  Time:     Circuit breakers + timeouts
  Location: Redundancy + failover across zones
  = A fire, a flood, or a swamped dependency stays contained.
```
